    "ls|LS16_S14_U_412": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA\nMINISTRY OF FINANCE\nDEPARTMENT OF FINANCIAL SERVICES\nLOK SABHA\nSTARRED QUESTION NO. *412\nTO BE ANSWERED ON THE 23RD MARCH 2018/ CHAITRA 2, 1939 (SAKA) \nSmall Finance Banks\n*412.   SHRI ARVIND SAWANT:\n   SHRI KRUPAL BALAJI TUMANE:\nWill the Minister of FINANCE be pleased to state:\n(a) whether the Government has granted permission to small finance banks to \nstart operations and if so, the details thereof;  \n(b) the details of the norms/criteria fixed for grant of micro finance status to \nthe said banks;\n(c) whether the small finance banks are adopting an aggressive strategy to \nattract more saving deposits by offering higher rate of interest than other banks; \nand\n(d)  if so, the details thereof and the reasons therefore? \nANSWER\nTHE FINANCE MINISTER\n(SHRI ARUN JAITLEY)\n(a) to (d):  A Statement is laid on the Table of the House.\n***\n\nSTATEMENT FOR LOK SABHA STARRED QUESTION NO. *412 FOR 23RD  \nMARCH 2018 REGARDING “SMALL FINANCE BANKS” BY SHRI ARVIND \nSAWANT AND SHRI KRUPAL BALAJI TUMANE, MEMBERS OF \nPARLIAMENT. \n(a) to (d): As per inputs of the Reserve Bank of India (RBI), banking licence, under \nsection 22(1) of the Banking Regulation Act, 1949, has been issued to 10 Small Finance \nBanks (SFBs), out of which nine have started operations. \nAs per RBI inputs, the guidelines for SFBs provide, interalia, that (i) eligible promoters \ncould be resident individuals/professionals with 10 years of banking and finance \nexperience, including companies controlled by them, etc.; (ii) SFBs shall primarily \nundertake basic banking activities of acceptance of deposits and lending to un-served and \nunder-served sections; (iii) the minimum paid-up equity capital for SFBs shall be Rs. 100 \ncrore; and (iv) SFBs shall be subject to all prudential norms and regulations of RBI, as \napplicable to existing commercial banks, including requirement of maintenance of Cash \nReserve Ratio and Statutory Liquidity Ratio. \nWith regard to whether SFBs are adopting an aggressive strategy to attract more saving \ndeposits by offering higher rate of interest than other banks and details and reasons \ntherefor, RBI has stated that it has deregulated the interest rates on savings bank account \nand banks have been given the freedom to fix their interest rates on savings bank account \nsubject to the conditions contained in RBI’s Master Directions on Interest Rates on \nDeposits. As per these Master Directions, banks are required to pay interest on deposits \nof money as per a comprehensive Board-approved policy on interest rates on deposits. \n***",
    "ls|LS16_S14_U_413": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION No.413  \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.02.2018 \n \nHigher Educational Institutions \n413. SHRI M. RAJA MOHAN REDDY:  \n        Will the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has any proposal to involve students pursuing Ph.D with \nsome financial support to teach some courses at the degree level in view of shortage of \nteaching staff and the delays in key recruitments in higher educational institutions; and  \n(b) if so, the details thereof? \nANSWER  \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n(DR. SATYA PAL SINGH)  \n \n(a) & (b): No such proposal is under consideration to involve students pursuing Ph.D to \nteach courses at degree level. However, in mo st of the Indian Institutes of Technology \n(IITs) / National Institutes of Technology (NITs), they are involved in teaching learning \nprocess as teaching assistants or laboratory assistants. Further, to overcome shortage of \nteaching staff educational institutions can hi re the services of guest faculty who possess \nthe minimum qualification for the post of Assistant Professor, as and when necessary.  \nHigher Educational Institutions may also access eminent teachers and researchers who \nhave completed their formal association with  the University/College, to participate in \nteaching, to collaborate and to stimulate research activities for quality research at M. Phil \nand Ph.D. levels. These teachers /researchers can also monitor the students as per the \nUniversity Grants Commission’s guidelines for empanelment of Adjunct Faculty in \nUniversities and Colleges. \n****** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S14_U_414": "LOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.414 \nFOR ANSWER ON 05/02/2018 \n \nPROMOTION OF STEEL SECTOR \n \n414. SHRI K.N. RAMACHANDRAN: \nSHRI BHARATHI MOHAN R.K.: \nSHRIMATI V. SATHYA BAMA: \nSHRI PR. SENTHIL NATHAN: \n \nWill the Minister of STEEL be pleased to state: \n \n(a) whether the Government has any  plans to develop and diversify the steel  \ncompanies under the Steel Authority of  India (SAIL) and also to encourage the  \nprivate players in this field and if so, the details thereof; \n \n(b) whether the domestic steel industry  is facing acute shortage of iron ore and  \nprocessed steel; \n \n(c) if so, the details thereof along with the corrective steps being taken to meet the \ngrowing need of high quality iron ore and processed steel in the domestic market; \n \n(d) whether the Government proposes  to formulate any exclusive policy or norms  \nfor used and reprocessed iron from scraps for domestic industry; and \n \n(e) if so, the details thereof along with the Government stand thereon? \n \nANSWER \nTHE MINISTER OF STATE FOR STEEL         (SHRI VISHNU DEO SAI) \n \n(a) Government has given “in -principle” approval for strategic disinvestment of \nVisvesvararya Steel Plant (VISP), Salem Steel Plant and Alloy Steel Plant, Durgapur \nof SAIL in order to bring technology, new management practices, funds for optimum \ndevelopment of business potential and growth of these Plants. Further, under \ndiversification plan, SAIL  and Arcelor Mittal signed an Mo U on May 22, 2015 to \nexplore the possibility of setting up an automotive steel manufacturing facility through \na Joint Venture (JV), in India.  \n \n(b) There is no acute shortage of iron ore and processed steel in the country.  \n \n(c) Does not arise. \n \n(d) No, Madam. \n \n(e) Does not arise. \n \n******** \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S14_U_415": "pGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n \nLOK SABHA \n \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 415 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.02.2018 \n \nFORMAL SKILLS \n \n415. SHRI DINESH TRIVEDI:   \n         \nWill the Minister of SKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRE NEURSHIP \nbe pleased to state: \n \n(a)  the rate of job growth in the formal and infor mal sectors of the Indian \nEconomy in the year 2017-18;  \n(b)  the percentage of workforce with formal skills  in India today;  \n(c) the measures taken by the Government to create formal skills in the labor \nforce and the success rate of these measures;  \n(d) whether the Government has taken cognizance of the fact that India stands at \nRank 103, lower than the BRICS nations, amongst 130  countries on the Global \nHuman Capital Index and if so, the details thereof; and \n(e)  whether the Government has taken/ is planning to take any new measures in \nresponse to this low ranking and if so, the details thereof?  \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF  \nSKILL DEVELOPMENT AND ENTREPRENEURSHIP \n(SHRI ANANTKUMAR HEGDE) \n(a) & (b)  As per National Sample Survey Office report “Statu s of Education and \nVocational Training in India”, 2011-12, among perso ns of age 15-59 years, about \n2.2% have received formal vocational training and 8 .6% have received non-formal \nvocational training. \n As per the Labour Bureau report “Fifth Annual Empl oyment – Unemployment \nSurvey (2015-16)”, 47.8% of the persons aged 15 years & above were reported to be \nemployed during the reference period of the survey.  The survey results showed that \nmajority of the persons were employed in the primary sector.  \n(c) to (e)  The Global Human Capital Report, 2017 brought out by the World \nEconomic Forum (WEF), using their own methodology, ranks 130 countries on how \n\nwell they are developing their human capital. The r eport has ranked India lower than \nother BRICS nations. \n The Government is implementing a number of schemes  for imparting \nemployable skills to the youth through short term a nd long term training. Under \nPradhan Mantri Kaushal Vikas Yojana (PMKVY), the fl agship scheme of the Ministry \nof Skill Development and Entrepreneurship (MSDE), a bout 34.9 lakh (about 27.8 \nlakhs candidates under fresh training and about 7.2  lakhs under Recognition of Prior \nLearning) candidates have been trained so far. Init iative has also been taken to set \nup Model Skill Centres called Pradhan Mantri Kausha l Kendras (PMKKs), in districts. \nAs on 15.12.2017, 527 PMKKs have been allocated to 484 districts throughout the \ncountry.  \n \nIndustrial Training Institutes (ITIs) infrastructur e has been strengthened to \nimpart long term training. There are 13912 ITIs in the country with seating capacity of \n29.6 lakh. \n \nIn order to promote apprenticeship, thereby creating industry-ready workforce, \nthe Government has launched the National Apprentice ship Promotion Scheme \n(NAPS) on 19th August 2016 under which 2.92 lakh ap prentices have been \nengaged/trained during 2016-17 and 2017-18.  \n \nConvergence with more than 20 Central Ministries/De partments implementing \nskill development programmes has been initiated. Un der this initiative, 90.78 lakh \npeople have so far been trained under various progr ammes of these \nMinistries/Departments in 2016-17. \n \n****** \n \n \n \n \n \n \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S14_U_416": "GOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF TOURISM \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO.†416 \nANSWERED ON 05.02.2018 \n \nSCHEMES FOR DEVELOPMENT OF TOURISM \n \n†416. COL. SONARAM CHOUDHARY:  \n \nWill the Minister of TOURISM be pleased to state:  \n \n(a)  the details of the schemes be ing implemented by the \nGovernment for developing tourism as an industry in the \ncountry;  \n(b)  the State/UT-wise details of the funds allocated and spent by \nthe Government for the promotion of tourism during each of \nthe last three years and the current year; and  \n(c)  whether the Government is formulating/proposes to formulate \nany scheme for developing desert circuits along with the \nalready existing religious circuits and if so, the details \nthereof? \nANSWER \n \nMINISTER OF STATE FOR TOURISM (INDEPENDENT CHARGE) \n(SHRI K.J. ALPHONS) \n \n(a): Major Schemes of the Ministry of Tourism for developing \ntourism sector in the country are: \n \n1) Integrated Development of Theme Based Tourist Circuits \n– Swadesh Darshan. \n2) National Mission on Pilgrimage Rejuvenation and \nSpiritual, Heritage Augmentation Drive (PRASHAD). \n3) Assistance to Central Agencies for Tourism \nInfrastructure Development. \n4) Domestic Promotion and Publicity including Hospitality. \n5) Restructured Scheme of Overseas Promotion & Publicity \nIncluding Market Development Assistance (MDA). \n\n6) Assistance to Institutes of Hotel Management/Food Craft \nInstitutes etc. \n7) Capacity Building for Service Providers. \n \n(b): Ministry of Tourism promotes India as a holistic tourism \ndestination including the various tourism sites and p roducts of \ndifferent States/Union Territories in the domestic and international \nmarkets. Ministry of Tourism grants Central Financial Assistance to \nStates/Union Territories for organizing fairs/festivals and tourism \nrelated events subject to the proposals meeting the provisions of \nthe guidelines. Details of funds sanctioned and released to State \nGovernments/Union Territory Administrations for organizing \nfairs/festivals and tourism related events during the last three years \nand the current year is at Annexure. \n \n(c): Ministry of Tourism has launched Swadesh Darshan Scheme \nfor development of theme based circuits in country along with \nPRASHAD Scheme for holistic development of identified pilgrimage \ndestinations. \n \n A total of fifteen themes including Desert Circu it have been \nidentified under the Swadesh Darshan Scheme for developing theme \nbased circuits spanning the entire country. Details of the project \nsanctioned under the Desert Circuit is: \n \n(Rs. in Crore) \nState Name of the Project Amt. Sanctioned \n2015-16 \nRajasthan Development of Sambhar Lake Town \nand Other Destinations in Rajasthan \nunder Desert Circuit. \n63.96 \n \n******\n\nANNEXURE \n \nSTATEMENT IN REPLY TO PART (b) OF LOK SABHA UNSTARRED \nQUESTION NO.†416 ANSWERED ON 05.02.2018 REGARDING \nSCHEMES FOR DEVELOPMENT OF TOURISM. \n \nDetails of funds sanctioned and released to State \nGovernments/Union Territory Administrations for organizing \nfairs/festivals and tourism related events during the last three years \nand the current year \n \n2014-15           (Rs. in Lakh) \nSl. \nNo. \nName of the \nState/UT  \nNo. of \nProjects \nAmount \nSanctioned \nAmount \nReleased \n1. Andhra Pradesh 03 30.00 30.00 \n2. Arunachal Pradesh 02 55.35 47.08 \n3. Chhattisgarh 01 50.00 50.00 \n4. Haryana 02 60.00 60.00 \n5. Madhya Pradesh 01 33.00 33.00 \n6. Manipur 01 50.00 50.00 \n7. Mizoram 02 50.00 50.00 \n8. Nagaland 01 25.00 25.00 \n9. Punjab 02 50.00 50.00 \n10. Rajasthan 01 40.00 40.00 \n11. Telangana 02 50.00 50.00 \n \n2015-16           (Rs. in Lakh)  \nSl. \nNo. \nName of the \nState/UT  \nNo. of \nProjects \nAmount \nSanctioned \nAmount \nReleased \n1. Arunachal Pradesh 03 30.00 30.00 \n2. Haryana 02 64.50 49.50 \n3. Kerala 01 12.50 12.50 \n4. Madhya Pradesh 01 19.00 19.00 \n5. Manipur 02 50.00 50.00 \n6. Mizoram 02 50.00 50.00 \n7. Punjab 02 32.50 32.50 \n8. Sikkim 02 44.00 44.00 \n9. Tamil Nadu 01 8.00 8.00 \n \n\n2016-17           (Rs. in Lakh)  \nSl. \nNo. \nName of the State/UT No. of \nProjects \nAmount \nSanctioned \nAmount \nReleased \n1. Haryana 01 30.00 30.00 \n2. Chandigarh  03 30.00 30.00 \n3. Andhra Pradesh 01 25.00 25.00 \n4. Kerala 02 50.00 50.00 \n5. Madhya Pradesh 02 42.00 42.00 \n6. Uttar Pradesh 02 55.00 55.00 \n7. Manipur 02 50.00 50.00 \n8. Meghalaya 02 42.22 42.22 \n9. Nagaland 01 25.00 25.00 \n10. Sikkim 02 50.00 50.00 \n11. Tripura 01 18.00 18.00 \n \n2017-18           (Rs. in Lakh)  \nSl. \nNo. \nName of the State/UT  No. of \nProjects \nAmount \nSanctioned \nAmount \nReleased \n1. Gujrat 01 25.00 25.00 \n2. Kerala 03 50.00 50.00 \n3. Himachal Pradesh 01 25.00 25.00 \n4. Chandigarh 03 30.00 30.00 \n5. Punjab 02 20.00 20.00 \n6. Telangana 01 20.00 20.00 \n7. Tamil Nadu 01 25.00 25.00 \n8. Haryana 02 55.00 40.00 \n9. Sikkim 02 50.00 50.00 \n10. Arunachal Pradesh 03 40.00 40.00 \n11. Assam 03 35.00 35.00 \n12. Nagaland 03 50.00 50.00 \n13. Manipur 02 50.00 50.00 \n14. Meghalaya 03 50.00 50.00 \n15. Mizoram 01 25.00 25.00 \n16. Uttar Pradesh 02 50.00 50.00 \n17. Madhya Pradesh 03 50.00 50.00 \n \n************ \n ",
    "ls|LS16_S14_U_417": " \n \nGOVERNMENT OF INDIA \nMINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \nDEPARTMENT OF HIGHER EDUCATION \n \nLOK SABHA \nUNSTARRED QUESTION NO. 417 \nTO BE ANSWERED ON 05.02.2018 \n \nNumber of Universities \n 417. SHRI PRALHAD JOSHI:  \nWill the Minister of HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT be pleased to state:  \n(a)  whether the number of universities in the country has gone up during the last three years; \n(b)       if so, the details thereof; \n(c) whether a number of students en rolled in higher studies such as\n undergraduate, postgraduate \nand research level studies has also gone up; and \n(d) if so, the details thereof? \n\t\nA N S W E R \nMINISTER OF STATE IN THE MINISTRY OF HUMAN RESOURCE DEVELOPMENT \n( DR. SATYA PAL SINGH ) \n(a) & (b) As per the All India Survey on High er Education (AISHE) 2016-17, the number of \nuniversities in the country has gon e up during last three years as evid ent from the figures in the table \nbelow for the period 2014-15, 2015-16 and 2016-17: \nYear Number of Universities \n2014-15 760 \n2015-16 799 \n2016-17 864 \n \n(c) & (d) As per All India Survey on Higher Edu cation (AISHE) 2016-17, the enrolment of students \nin higher education has gone up from 2.92 crore in 2011-12 to 3.57 crore in 2016-17. \n \n********* ",
